"""Long-form original guides for Brand.bz (slug, title, description, category, minutes, html)."""

ARTICLES = [
("learn-what-is-a-brand", "What Is a Brand? Brand vs. Branding vs. Brand Identity", "A clear, practical explanation of the difference between a brand, branding and brand identity — and why small businesses should care.", "Foundations", 8, """
<p>Ask ten people what a brand is and you will hear ten answers: a logo, a name, a color, a reputation. All of them are pieces of the picture, but none of them is the whole thing. Getting the definition right is not academic — it changes where you spend your time and money.</p>
<h2 id="brand">1. A brand is a perception</h2>
<p>A brand is the set of expectations, memories and feelings that people associate with a business, product or person. It lives in the customer's head, not in your design files. You do not fully own your brand; you influence it. Every interaction — the website, the packaging, how quickly you answer an email, what a friend says about you — adds a deposit or a withdrawal.</p>
<p>That is why two companies with near-identical products can command very different prices. Customers are not only buying the item; they are buying the confidence, status or simplicity that the brand stands for.</p>
<h2 id="branding">2. Branding is the work</h2>
<p>Branding is the deliberate, ongoing effort to shape that perception. It includes strategy (who you serve and why you are different), expression (name, visuals, voice) and experience (the way your product and service actually feel). Branding is a verb. It never really ends, because markets, competitors and customers keep changing.</p>
<h2 id="identity">3. Brand identity is the toolkit</h2>
<p>Brand identity is the collection of tangible assets you control: your name, logo, color palette, typography, imagery style, tagline and tone of voice. Identity is how you <em>express</em> the brand. A strong identity is consistent and recognizable — people can spot your social post before they read the account name.</p>
<table><tr><th>Term</th><th>What it is</th><th>Who controls it</th></tr><tr><td>Brand</td><td>The perception in people's minds</td><td>Customers (influenced by you)</td></tr><tr><td>Branding</td><td>The process of shaping that perception</td><td>You and your team</td></tr><tr><td>Brand identity</td><td>The visible and verbal assets</td><td>You</td></tr></table>
<h2 id="why">4. Why this matters for small businesses</h2>
<p>Small businesses often start with a logo because it feels concrete. But a logo built before the strategy is a guess. A better order is:</p>
<ol><li><b>Define your audience.</b> Be specific: "independent yoga teachers in their first two years" beats "people who like fitness."</li><li><b>Write your positioning.</b> One sentence explaining what you do, for whom, and why you are different. Try our <a href="positioning.html">Positioning Statement Builder</a>.</li><li><b>Choose a name and voice</b> that fit that positioning. Our <a href="name-generator.html">Brand Name Generator</a> can kick-start ideas.</li><li><b>Design the identity</b> — colors, fonts, logo — to express it.</li><li><b>Deliver the experience</b> consistently, then measure and refine.</li></ol>
<h2 id="mistakes">5. Common mistakes</h2>
<ul><li><b>Confusing a logo with a brand.</b> A beautiful mark cannot rescue a confusing offer.</li><li><b>Trying to appeal to everyone.</b> Broad brands are forgettable brands.</li><li><b>Inconsistency.</b> Five shades of blue and three fonts tell customers you are not paying attention.</li><li><b>Ignoring the experience.</b> Slow replies and messy invoices are branding too.</li></ul>
<blockquote>Rule of thumb: if you removed your logo from everything you publish, would people still recognize it as yours? If yes, you are building a brand, not just a logo.</blockquote>
<h2 id="next">6. Your next step</h2>
<p>Take the free <a href="brand-score.html">Brand Strength Score</a> quiz. In two minutes you will see which of the twelve core brand foundations you already have and which need attention.</p>
"""),
("learn-how-to-name-a-business", "How to Name a Business: A 7-Step Framework That Works", "A step-by-step naming process — name types, brainstorming techniques, screening tests, domains and trademark checks.", "Naming", 10, """
<p>A name is the most-used asset your brand will ever have. It appears in every conversation, search, invoice and referral. Changing it later is expensive, so a disciplined process up front pays for itself many times over.</p>
<h2>Step 1 — Write a naming brief</h2>
<p>Before brainstorming, answer five questions on one page: What do we do? Who is it for? What three feelings should the name evoke? Who are the main competitors and what are they called? Where will we operate (local, national, global)? The brief keeps the team from arguing over taste and lets you judge ideas against criteria.</p>
<h2>Step 2 — Understand the main name types</h2>
<table><tr><th>Type</th><th>How it works</th><th>Trade-off</th></tr><tr><td>Descriptive</td><td>Says what you do ("Fresh Bakes")</td><td>Clear, but hard to protect and easy to copy</td></tr><tr><td>Suggestive</td><td>Hints at a benefit or feeling</td><td>Good balance of meaning and distinctiveness</td></tr><tr><td>Compound</td><td>Two words fused together</td><td>Memorable; check that the join reads cleanly</td></tr><tr><td>Invented</td><td>A new word with no prior meaning</td><td>Highly protectable; needs more marketing to build meaning</td></tr><tr><td>Founder / personal</td><td>Uses a person's name</td><td>Personal and trustworthy; harder to sell the business later</td></tr><tr><td>Acronym</td><td>Initials</td><td>Short, but usually forgettable for new brands</td></tr></table>
<h2>Step 3 — Brainstorm wide</h2>
<p>Aim for at least 100 candidates before judging any. Useful techniques:</p>
<ul><li><b>Word banks:</b> list words for your product, benefits, feelings, places and metaphors.</li><li><b>Blending:</b> merge the start of one word with the end of another.</li><li><b>Affixes:</b> add short prefixes or suffixes ("-ly", "-ify", "-hub").</li><li><b>Other languages:</b> explore Latin, Greek or the languages of your market — and verify meanings with native speakers.</li><li><b>Generators:</b> use our <a href="name-generator.html">Brand Name Generator</a> to produce dozens of combinations in seconds.</li></ul>
<h2>Step 4 — Screen with the five tests</h2>
<ol><li><b>Radio test:</b> say it out loud once. Can someone spell it correctly?</li><li><b>Length test:</b> two to three syllables and under ten letters is ideal.</li><li><b>Meaning test:</b> does it have negative meanings in key languages or slang?</li><li><b>Distinctiveness test:</b> is it clearly different from competitors?</li><li><b>Stretch test:</b> will it still fit if you add products or enter new markets?</li></ol>
<p>Our <a href="name-checker.html">Name Strength Checker</a> scores length, syllables and pronounceability automatically.</p>
<h2>Step 5 — Check domains and social handles</h2>
<p>An exact-match .com is still the most trusted, but strong alternatives exist: short country codes, industry extensions, or adding a verb ("get", "try"). Secure matching handles on the social platforms your customers actually use on the same day.</p>
<h2>Step 6 — Run a trademark knock-out search</h2>
<p>Search official databases for identical and similar marks in your class of goods or services: the USPTO (United States), CIPO (Canada), EUIPO (European Union) and WIPO's Global Brand Database (international). A clean search is not a guarantee — a trademark professional can run a full clearance — but it filters out obvious conflicts early. Read our <a href="learn-trademark-basics.html">trademark basics guide</a>.</p>
<h2>Step 7 — Test with real people</h2>
<p>Show your top three names to people in your target audience. Ask what kind of business each sounds like and which they would remember tomorrow. Then call them the next day and ask which ones they recall. Memory is the real test.</p>
<blockquote>Shortcut: if you are torn between two finalists, pick the one that is easier to say over the phone. Word of mouth is still the cheapest marketing there is.</blockquote>
"""),
("learn-brand-color-psychology", "Brand Colors: Psychology, Palettes and the 60-30-10 Rule", "How to choose brand colors that communicate the right emotion, stay accessible and remain consistent everywhere.", "Visual Identity", 9, """
<p>Color is often the first thing people notice and the last thing they forget. Done well, a palette makes your brand recognizable in a fraction of a second. Done poorly, it creates noise, hurts readability and makes your marketing look accidental.</p>
<h2>How color associations work</h2>
<p>Colors do not carry fixed meanings — culture, context and competitors all matter — but there are broad associations worth knowing:</p>
<table><tr><th>Color</th><th>Common associations</th><th>Watch out for</th></tr><tr><td>Blue</td><td>Trust, calm, reliability</td><td>Crowded in finance and tech</td></tr><tr><td>Green</td><td>Growth, health, nature, money</td><td>Can feel generic in wellness</td></tr><tr><td>Red</td><td>Energy, urgency, passion</td><td>Can signal danger or errors</td></tr><tr><td>Orange</td><td>Friendly, affordable, playful</td><td>May read as cheap if overused</td></tr><tr><td>Yellow</td><td>Optimism, attention</td><td>Poor contrast on white</td></tr><tr><td>Purple</td><td>Creativity, premium, imagination</td><td>Varies widely by culture</td></tr><tr><td>Black</td><td>Luxury, sophistication, authority</td><td>Can feel cold without warm accents</td></tr></table>
<h2>Build a palette with roles, not just colors</h2>
<p>A working brand palette assigns every color a job:</p>
<ul><li><b>Primary</b> — your signature color, used for the logo and the most important actions.</li><li><b>Secondary</b> — supports the primary and adds depth.</li><li><b>Accent</b> — used sparingly to highlight.</li><li><b>Neutrals</b> — light backgrounds and dark text colors that carry most of the layout.</li></ul>
<h2>The 60-30-10 rule</h2>
<p>Borrowed from interior design, the rule suggests about 60% of a layout uses a dominant neutral or base color, 30% uses a secondary color and 10% uses an accent. It prevents the "everything is shouting" effect and keeps your call-to-action buttons visible.</p>
<h2>Harmony models</h2>
<ul><li><b>Analogous:</b> neighboring hues — calm and cohesive.</li><li><b>Complementary:</b> opposite hues — high energy and contrast.</li><li><b>Triadic:</b> three evenly spaced hues — vibrant and balanced.</li><li><b>Monochrome:</b> one hue in different lightness levels — minimal and elegant.</li></ul>
<p>Try each model in our free <a href="color-palette.html">Brand Color Palette Generator</a>; you can lock colors you like and shuffle the rest.</p>
<h2>Accessibility is not optional</h2>
<p>Text should meet a contrast ratio of at least 4.5:1 against its background for normal body copy (WCAG AA). Large headlines can use 3:1. Our palette tool shows whether each swatch passes with white or dark text. Accessible brands reach more people and generally convert better because content is easier to read.</p>
<h2>Document and enforce</h2>
<p>Record exact HEX, RGB and CMYK values in a one-page guide. Our <a href="brand-guide.html">Brand Guide Builder</a> pulls in your saved palette automatically and lets you print the result to PDF.</p>
"""),
("learn-positioning-statement", "How to Write a Brand Positioning Statement (With Templates)", "Write a clear positioning statement that guides your marketing, plus the 'onliness' test and real-world style examples.", "Strategy", 7, """
<p>A positioning statement is an internal sentence that defines who you serve, what you offer and why you are the better choice. Customers rarely see it, but it shapes everything they do see — your headline, your pricing, your ads and your product roadmap.</p>
<h2>The classic template</h2>
<blockquote>For <b>[target customer]</b> who <b>[need or problem]</b>, <b>[brand]</b> is the <b>[category]</b> that <b>[key benefit]</b>. Unlike <b>[main alternative]</b>, we <b>[unique difference]</b>.</blockquote>
<p>Each blank forces a decision. If you cannot fill one in, you have found the weak spot in your strategy.</p>
<h2>Filling in each part</h2>
<ul><li><b>Target customer:</b> narrow enough to picture a real person. Include context, not only demographics.</li><li><b>Need:</b> the job they are trying to get done or the frustration they feel.</li><li><b>Category:</b> the frame of reference customers already understand ("bookkeeping app", "coffee subscription").</li><li><b>Key benefit:</b> the outcome, not the feature. "Close your books in 10 minutes" beats "automated reconciliation."</li><li><b>Alternative:</b> what customers would do without you — including doing nothing.</li><li><b>Difference:</b> the reason to believe. It must be true, provable and meaningful to the customer.</li></ul>
<h2>The onliness test</h2>
<p>A simpler, sharper variation asks you to complete: "Our brand is the <i>only</i> [category] that [difference] for [audience]." If the sentence feels uncomfortable or untrue, your differentiation is not yet strong enough — or you are trying to serve too many people.</p>
<h2>Examples (fictional brands)</h2>
<ul><li>For first-time home cooks who feel overwhelmed by recipes, <b>Panly</b> is the meal-kit service that teaches one technique per box. Unlike standard meal kits, we focus on skills, so customers cook confidently without us.</li><li>For freelance designers who lose hours chasing invoices, <b>Paidpath</b> is the invoicing tool that follows up automatically in the designer's own voice. Unlike generic accounting apps, every reminder sounds human.</li></ul>
<h2>Turn it into customer-facing copy</h2>
<p>Your homepage headline is usually the key benefit; the subheading names the audience and difference. Test the statement with your team: if everyone can repeat it from memory, it is working.</p>
<p>Build yours in seconds with the <a href="positioning.html">Positioning Statement Builder</a>.</p>
"""),
("learn-brand-voice", "Brand Voice: How to Sound Like Yourself Everywhere", "Define a distinctive brand voice with traits, do/don't lists and channel-by-channel tone adjustments.", "Messaging", 7, """
<p>If your visual identity is how your brand looks, your voice is how it sounds. A consistent voice makes a five-person company feel established and makes a large company feel human.</p>
<h2>Voice vs. tone</h2>
<p><b>Voice</b> is your consistent personality. <b>Tone</b> is how that personality adapts to the situation. You speak differently at a celebration and at a funeral, but you are still you. Likewise, a brand might be playful on social media and calm and clear in a billing error message.</p>
<h2>Step 1 — Choose three voice traits</h2>
<p>Pick three adjectives that describe how you want to sound — for example <i>confident, warm, plain-spoken</i>. Three is enough to be distinctive and few enough to remember.</p>
<h2>Step 2 — Define each trait with do's and don'ts</h2>
<table><tr><th>Trait</th><th>We are</th><th>We are not</th></tr><tr><td>Confident</td><td>Direct, decisive, specific</td><td>Arrogant or dismissive</td></tr><tr><td>Warm</td><td>Friendly, encouraging</td><td>Overly casual or gushing</td></tr><tr><td>Plain-spoken</td><td>Short sentences, everyday words</td><td>Jargon-heavy or vague</td></tr></table>
<h2>Step 3 — Write sample lines</h2>
<p>Rewrite five common messages in your voice: a welcome email, an error message, a sale announcement, an apology and a social caption. These examples teach new team members faster than any rule.</p>
<h2>Step 4 — Adjust tone by channel</h2>
<ul><li><b>Website:</b> clear and benefit-led.</li><li><b>Social:</b> more relaxed, more personality.</li><li><b>Support:</b> calm, empathetic, solution first.</li><li><b>Sales proposals:</b> confident, evidence-based.</li></ul>
<h2>Step 5 — Keep a word list</h2>
<p>List words you always use (your product terms, signature phrases) and words you never use (jargon, competitor phrases, anything off-brand). Add it to your <a href="brand-guide.html">brand guide</a>.</p>
<blockquote>Quick check: cover your logo and read a piece of your content. Could it have been written by a competitor? If so, your voice needs more personality.</blockquote>
<p>Need tagline ideas that match your voice? Try the <a href="slogan-generator.html">Slogan Generator</a>.</p>
"""),
("learn-trademark-basics", "Trademark Basics for New Brands (Plain-English Guide)", "General information on trademarks for founders — what they protect, how to search, and when to call a professional.", "Protection", 8, """
<p class="muted"><i>This guide is general information, not legal advice. Laws differ by country; consult a qualified trademark lawyer or agent for your situation.</i></p>
<p>A trademark protects the names, logos and slogans that identify the source of goods or services. For a new brand, understanding the basics can prevent a painful forced rebrand after you have invested in signage, packaging and marketing.</p>
<h2>What a trademark does — and does not — protect</h2>
<ul><li><b>Protects:</b> brand names, logos, taglines, and sometimes distinctive colors, sounds or packaging, when they identify the source of a product or service.</li><li><b>Does not protect:</b> general ideas, common descriptive words used in their ordinary meaning, or business methods. Copyright covers creative works; patents cover inventions.</li></ul>
<h2>The distinctiveness spectrum</h2>
<p>Marks are often described on a spectrum from weakest to strongest: <b>generic</b> (the common name of the product — not protectable), <b>descriptive</b> (describes a quality — hard to protect without proof of recognition), <b>suggestive</b> (hints at a benefit), <b>arbitrary</b> (a real word unrelated to the product) and <b>fanciful</b> (an invented word). The further right you go, the easier protection generally becomes.</p>
<h2>Searching before you commit</h2>
<p>Run free preliminary searches in the official databases:</p>
<ul><li>United States — USPTO trademark search</li><li>Canada — Canadian Intellectual Property Office (CIPO)</li><li>European Union — EUIPO eSearch</li><li>International — WIPO Global Brand Database</li></ul>
<p>Search exact spellings, phonetic variants and similar meanings within the classes of goods and services you plan to offer. Our <a href="name-checker.html">Name Strength Checker</a> generates direct search links for any name.</p>
<h2>Registration in brief</h2>
<p>Most countries use the Nice Classification system of 45 classes. You file an application listing the goods or services, an examiner reviews it, it is published for opposition, and if all goes well it registers. Timelines commonly range from several months to over a year. International protection can be extended through the Madrid System administered by WIPO.</p>
<h2>Using the symbols</h2>
<p>™ (or ℠ for services) is generally used to claim rights in a mark that is not registered. ® is typically reserved for marks registered with the relevant national office; misuse can carry penalties in some jurisdictions.</p>
<h2>When to bring in a professional</h2>
<ul><li>Before spending significantly on launch materials</li><li>When search results show similar marks in related classes</li><li>When expanding to new countries</li><li>If you receive a cease-and-desist letter</li></ul>
"""),
("learn-brand-launch-checklist", "The 40-Point Brand Launch Checklist", "Everything to prepare before launching a new brand — strategy, identity, digital, legal and launch-day tasks.", "Launch", 6, """
<p>Use this checklist to make sure nothing important is missing on launch day. Print it, or bookmark it and tick items off as you go.</p>
<h2>Strategy (1–8)</h2>
<ol><li>Ideal customer profile written</li><li>Customer problems and desired outcomes listed</li><li>Positioning statement completed (<a href="positioning.html">builder</a>)</li><li>Three to five competitors analysed</li><li>Core brand promise defined</li><li>Mission and values drafted</li><li>Pricing position chosen (budget, mid, premium)</li><li>Brand story written in three short paragraphs</li></ol>
<h2>Name & protection (9–14)</h2>
<ol start="9"><li>Name shortlist tested with real customers</li><li>Preliminary trademark searches completed</li><li>Primary domain registered</li><li>Typo and alternate-extension domains considered</li><li>Social handles secured</li><li>Business registration aligned with brand name</li></ol>
<h2>Identity (15–24)</h2>
<ol start="15"><li>Logo in full-color, one-color and reversed versions</li><li>Favicon and social profile icons</li><li>Color palette with HEX/RGB/CMYK values</li><li>Typography choices and fallbacks</li><li>Imagery and photography style</li><li>Voice traits with do's and don'ts</li><li>Tagline finalised</li><li>One-page brand guide (<a href="brand-guide.html">builder</a>)</li><li>Email signature template</li><li>Business card and invoice templates</li></ol>
<h2>Digital presence (25–34)</h2>
<ol start="25"><li>Website live with clear headline and call to action</li><li>About, Contact and Privacy pages published</li><li>Analytics installed</li><li>Business profile created on major maps and search platforms</li><li>Social bios consistent everywhere</li><li>Email list sign-up form working</li><li>Welcome email sequence written</li><li>Social post templates designed</li><li>Page speed and mobile layout tested</li><li>Accessibility basics checked (contrast, alt text)</li></ol>
<h2>Launch (35–40)</h2>
<ol start="35"><li>Launch announcement written</li><li>Friends, partners and early customers briefed</li><li>Launch offer or incentive prepared</li><li>Press or community outreach list built</li><li>Feedback form ready</li><li>30-day post-launch review scheduled</li></ol>
<p>Prefer an expert review of your checklist? <a href="audit.html">Request a free brand audit</a>.</p>
"""),
]
